 in your life it was I missed a lot  desperate to get out fincher consider  taking a deal before her evidence was  even tested by the crime lab her  attorney James Freeman says in Georgia  that  steam process could take at least three  months so there is a chance innocent  people are taking these plea deals just  to get out of jail it's not just a  chance it's a fact  Fincher passed on taking that deal and  was eventually released when the lab  results came back negative for any  illegal drugs so the backlog isn't just  about cases like daesh's it's not just  drug cases it's not just accused  criminals  it's everything that needs to be tested  by the crime lab but Georgia isn't the  only state facing a growing crime lab  backlog CBS News has uncovered dozens of  states are significantly behind Illinois  has a backlog of more than 23,000 cases  in South Carolina it could take more  than nine months to get results back on  DNA or Firearms testing in Idaho some  cases have been pending for almost two  years  is there a backlog here in Arkansas oh  absolutely  this is all evidence that's waiting to  go home Arkansas crime lab director  Kermit channel says the problem  escalated about six years ago coinciding  with the rise of the opioid crisis in  his lab alone DNA and drug cases have  nearly doubled since 2014 they are more  complex and if we talk about a drug case  it's not simple marijuana it's  methamphetamine its fentanyl it's heroin  this is this where it all happened this  is the laboratories where we have almost  twenty eight chemistry we were given  rare access inside Arkansas's crime lab  where scientists carefully test each  piece of evidence  it's a painstakingly meticulous process  I can smell the evidence in here you can  downstairs is where all the evidence is  stored each of these boxes holds  essential evidence inside them important  for a case and in drug cases alone there  are 23,000 of them coming through the  Arkansas crime lab a year so in Arkansas  I could take over 150 days to possibly  get that case in a position where they  could take it to a grand jury or get an  indictment yeah absolutely you know that  and that's concerning this isn't just an  Arkansas problem oh absolutely not I  mean this is this is a national epidemic  so Arkansas is crime lab actually our  Arkansas governor approved more  funding for their crime lab and the  state will actually open up a new lab in  July that should help it's closely  working with prosecutors to close out  cases where a deal has been made and  they are now farming out some of this  testing to private labs because they  just can't handle this backlog  themselves the number of people in  America not convicted of a crime but  still sitting in jail is appalling that  is exactly what is happening here and  not in all cases obviously but but but  clearly they're trying to clear this  backlog in the prosecution part of this  and also in the evidence part of this I  can't imagine what Daisha went through  with a million dollar bond on obvious  when she obviously couldn't pay she was  stuck you you say she really did almost  consider that deal she really she knew  she was innocent it was blue it was blue  cotton candy obviously and she just  wanted to get out she was so scared of  being in jail she's like I'll just I'll  just take this deal and that's what I  keep focusing on - Anthony is daesh's  story because it's all it's all kind of  levels of wrong number one I don't  understand why the bond is set at a  million dollars for that infraction you  know a lot of that had to do with the  intent to distribute and because it was  the amount that they claimed they  thought it was of meth and so that's a  big problem but you know a lot of people  talk about the rape kit backlog this is  a byproduct that is a byproduct of what  we are seeing in these crime labs around  the country very important story very  good didn't have you have the table and  see you in person I know thanks Maria  said in the green room look I'm wearing  girl pants in real  [Laughter]

